<Article
  title="Context: the long read"
  description="What an AI coding agent knows right now, why its context window fills up, what to give it, where standing instructions live, and the habits that keep a thread useful."
  section="explain"
  updated="2026-11-18"
  headings={headings}
  prev={{ href: 'explain/plans-and-api/', title: 'Plans against the API' }}
  next={{ href: 'explain/verification/', title: 'Verification' }}
>
  <h2 id="what-the-agent-can-see">What the agent can see</h2>
  <p>
    A coding agent does not see your screen, and it does not remember yesterday. Each time it takes a step it reads one long
    document from the top: its instructions, everything you have typed in this conversation, every file it has opened, the
    output of every command it has run, and its own earlier replies. That document is its
    <Term def="Everything the model reads before it writes its next reply: instructions, your messages, files it has opened, results of commands, its own earlier replies. Nothing outside it exists for the model.">context window</Term>.
    What is in it, the agent can use. What is not in it does not exist for the agent, however obvious it is to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Think of a capable contractor who arrives every morning with no memory of the day before and works only from the papers on
    the desk. If the papers say which page is broken and what right looks like, the work goes well. If they say "fix the
    holidays", the contractor guesses, and agents rarely stop to ask before guessing.
  </p>
  <p>
    A new conversation, which most tools call a thread, starts with an empty desk apart from the standing instructions. Close
    the thread and open another, and everything you explained in the first one is gone unless it was written into a file.
  </p>

  <Deeper level="technical" title="Tokens and window sizes">
    <p>
      Models read text in <Term def="A piece of text a few characters long, the unit models read, write and bill in. A short English word is usually one token; code costs more tokens than prose of the same length.">tokens</Term>,
      and the window is measured in them. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.defaultModel" show="claim" />
      Opus 5.5 has a context window of <Fact id="models.claude-opus-5-5.context" />. On the OpenAI side, <Fact id="models.gpt-6-2-sol.context" show="claim" />
      So the harness you use, not only the model, decides how much room a session has.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything the agent does goes into the window as text. Its instructions and tool descriptions sit at the top. Your
      messages are usually the smallest part. The large items are tool results: a file it read in full, a test run, a server
      log, a page of search results, a screenshot. A spreadsheet printed to the terminal can be hundreds of thousands of
      tokens on its own.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="the-window-fills-up">The window fills up</h2>
  <p>
    The window is large, but it is finite, and it fills as the agent works. Your prompt is usually under one per cent of
    it. What fills it is what the agent's tools hand back: files it read, test runs, logs, printed output. That is why a
    thread can feel fine for an hour and then go strange: nobody typed much, but the agent read a lot. The
    <a href={url('explain/context/')}>board</a> lets you fill a window yourself and watch it compact.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fuller window costs you in three ways. Every new turn sends the whole window to the model again, so each step is slower
    and uses more of your plan. The model has more to sift, including old attempts that failed and details from a task you
    finished an hour ago, and answers get worse well before the window is full. And when it is nearly full, the tool
    <Term def="Squeezing a long conversation into a short summary so the work can carry on. The summary keeps what the harness judges important; everything else is dropped.">compacts</Term>
    it: the conversation is replaced by a summary, and whatever the summary left out is gone. That might be the exact error
    message, a constraint you mentioned once, or why the first fix was abandoned.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Claude Code documentation is plain about it. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.focusedContext" show="claim" />
  </p>

  <Deeper level="technical" title="What fills it in Claude Code, and the commands that help">
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.startupContext" show="claim" /> Then each file read, each command and each reply adds to it. Run
      <code>/context</code> in a session to see the breakdown by category, with the instruction files that loaded. The
      <a href={links.claudeCodeContextWindow.url}>context window page</a> in the Claude Code docs plays a whole session as a timeline.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>/clear</code> starts a fresh conversation in the same session. Use it between unrelated tasks.</li>
      <li><code>/compact keep the API changes and the test commands</code> compacts now, with your say over what the summary keeps.</li>
      <li><code>/rewind</code> (or <kbd>Esc</kbd> twice) rolls the conversation, the code or both back to an earlier prompt, and can summarise part of a conversation that has gone stale.</li>
      <li><code>/autocompact</code> sets how full the window gets before automatic compaction. By default it runs at about <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.autoCompactAt" /> on models with a 1M window.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Large tool output is the thing to watch. Ask for a summary, a count or the first twenty lines rather than the whole file;
      ask for the failing tests rather than the full log.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Why quality drops before the window is full">
    <p>
      <Fact id="claims.research.contextRot" show="claim" /> The practical version: retrieval of one fact from a long context is
      less reliable than from a short one, and everything in the window competes for the model's attention, relevant or not.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things make a long thread worse than its length alone. It holds distractors: files from a finished task, superseded
      plans, an old version of the code the agent has since changed. And it holds failures. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.clearAfterTwo" show="claim" />
      The model treats its own earlier attempts as evidence about what to do next, so a thread that has gone wrong tends to stay wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      A bigger window only moves the point where this starts to bite. Size a task so one thread can finish it with room to
      spare, and treat compaction as a fallback.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Prompt caching and why a stable prefix keeps cost down" id="prompt-caching">
    <p>
      Each turn re-sends the whole conversation, so without caching a long session pays for its history again and again.
      Prompt caching stores the processed start of a prompt and reuses it. <Fact id="lineup.cacheExactPrefix" show="claim" />
      On the API, Opus 5.5 input costs <Fact id="models.claude-opus-5-5.price.input" /> per million tokens and a cache read
      <Fact id="models.claude-opus-5-5.price.cacheRead" />; writing to the cache costs <Fact id="lineup.cacheWriteMultiplier" />
      the input price. <Fact id="lineup.cacheDefaultTtl" show="claim" />
    </p>
    <p>
      Harnesses build the prompt in a fixed order for this reason: tool definitions, then the system prompt and instruction
      files, then the conversation, growing only at the end. Anything that changes the front of that order invalidates
      everything after it: editing the instruction file mid-session, connecting or removing a tool server, switching model.
      Appending a message at the end keeps the cache intact.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.promptCacheTtl" show="claim" /> A long, idle session that comes back after the cache expired pays full
      price for its whole history on the next turn, which is one more reason to clear between tasks.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Compaction: what survives and how to steer it" id="compaction">
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.compactionKeeps" show="claim" /> Instruction files in subdirectories and path-scoped rules
      load again only when the agent next touches a matching file, and anything that only existed in the conversation is
      reduced to whatever the summary kept.
    </p>
    <p>Ways to keep control:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Compact on purpose at a natural break, with a focus, rather than letting it happen mid-task.</li>
      <li>Before a long run, have the agent keep a progress file (what is done, what is next, decisions and why). After compaction, or in a new thread, it reads the file and carries on. This is the same structured note-taking Anthropic describes in <a href={links.anthropicContextEngineering.url}>its context engineering write-up</a>.</li>
      <li>Put rules that must survive in the root instruction file, not in a message.</li>
      <li>For a fresh start with a handover, ask for a short summary first, check it, then paste it into a new thread. You decide what crosses over.</li>
    </ul>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="context-is-capability">Context is capability</h2>
  <p>
    The same model, given the same sentence, does very different work depending on what else it can see. Send "The opening
    hours are wrong on holidays. Fix it." with nothing else, and the agent has to guess which days count as holidays, what
    the page should say on them and how to check the fix. Each guess can be wrong. The board shows the guesses for all three
    running examples, and which piece of context removes each one.
  </p>

  <p>Most bad results trace back to something the agent could not see. Before you send a task, check it has these:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The goal, in plain words: what is wrong or what you want, and who it is for.</li>
    <li>What done looks like: the check that proves it works, such as a test that passes or a page that shows the right thing.</li>
    <li>The relevant material: the file, the page, the error, the screenshot. Point at it or paste it in, rather than describe it.</li>
    <li>The constraints: what must not change, what it may not touch, anything you have already tried.</li>
    <li>An example, when you have one: a row of the spreadsheet as it should look, a page that already does it right.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You do not need all of it every time. A quick question needs a quick prompt. The longer the agent will work without you,
    the more of this it needs up front, because it will not come back to ask.
  </p>

  <h2 id="a-prompt-you-can-copy">A prompt you can copy</h2>
  <p>
    A good working prompt has three parts: what you want, what done looks like, and when to stop and ask. The third gives the
    agent permission to stop when it is unsure, which it otherwise tends not to take. Here it is for the holiday hours bug.
  </p>

  <CopyPrompt title="Fix the holiday hours bug" id="prompt-skeleton">{skeleton}</CopyPrompt>

  <p>
    The three-part shape comes from Anthropic's guidance on working with Opus 5.5, which Theo reads through and tries out in
    <a href={links.theoOpusPrompting.url}>Getting the most out of Opus 5.5</a> (25 September 2026). The part on "what done
    means" starts at about eleven minutes in.
  </p>

  <Embed id="ejjBbaq9RmY" title="Getting the most out of Opus 5.5, by Theo" start={669} />

  <h2 id="standing-instructions">Standing instructions live in a file</h2>
  <p>
    Some things are true of every task in a project: how to run the tests, where the holiday list lives, what must never be
    touched. Rather than type them every time, put them in a project instruction file. The agent reads it at the start of
    every thread. Codex reads a file called <code>AGENTS.md</code>; Claude Code reads <code>CLAUDE.md</code>,
    and falls back to <code>AGENTS.md</code> when there is none.
  </p>
  <p>Keep it short, and keep it to what the agent cannot work out by reading the code:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>How to check work: the exact commands for tests, type checks and linting.</li>
    <li>Things the code gets wrong or hides: an old pattern not to copy, a folder that looks unused and is not.</li>
    <li>Decisions that are not written anywhere else: how deploys work, who edits the holiday list.</li>
    <li>Anything you have had to correct twice.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Leave out what it can find for itself: a tour of the folders, general coding advice, long lists of style rules, and
    instructions that only matter for one task. Every line is read on every task, so a long file costs room and gets followed
    less reliably. A small one for the bakery site might look like this:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{agentsMd}</code></pre>

  <Deeper level="technical" title="How instruction files load and nest">
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.contextFile" show="claim" /> <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.claudeMdNesting" show="claim" />
      Codex works down the tree instead: <Fact id="harnesses.codex.contextFile" show="claim" />
    </p>
    <p>
      Either way, a file in a subfolder adds rules for that part of the project, and a file in your home folder applies to every
      project. Keep personal preferences there or in a local file that is not committed, so the project file stays about the
      project. Anthropic suggests a size: <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.claudeMdLines" show="claim" />
    </p>
    <p>
      The model follows a line in the file most of the time. A rule that must never be broken, such as "never push to main", belongs
      in the harness's permission settings or a hook, where it is enforced.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Retrieval, memory files and subagents as context firewalls" id="subagents">
    <p>
      Loading whole files is the expensive default. A better pattern is to load just in time: the agent keeps cheap pointers
      (paths, a grep, a test name) and reads the slice it needs when it needs it. Point the agent at the function, not the
      folder, and prefer commands that return a little, like <code>grep -n</code> or a single failing test, to ones that return a lot.
    </p>
    <p>
      Memory files carry state across threads. Claude Code keeps its own: <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.autoMemoryLoad" show="claim" />
      A plain progress file in the repo does the same job in any harness and is visible in review.
    </p>
    <p>
      A subagent is the strongest tool here. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagents" show="claim" /> It can read fifty files and
      return a one-paragraph answer, so the main thread receives the conclusion without the reading. That makes it a firewall
      for noise: big searches, log trawls and independent review belong there. It is not free. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentUsage" show="claim" />
      A fresh subagent also has to gather context the main thread already had, so for small jobs it can be slower than doing the work in place.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="habits">Habits that keep a thread useful</h2>
  <p>
    One task per thread. When the contact tidy is done, start a new thread for the club list. The new thread starts small and
    clean, and the old one is still there if you need to go back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start fresh rather than dragging a long thread on. If a thread has wandered, ask for a short summary of where things
    stand, open a new one and paste it in. It is quicker than steering a confused agent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Edit your last message and retry, instead of arguing. If the agent misread you, a correction adds a second message to a
    conversation that still holds the first misreading. Most tools let you edit the message you sent, or roll back to it, and
    run it again with the missing piece added. The bad attempt then never enters the window.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let helpers do the reading. When a task needs a lot of searching, ask the agent to hand the search to a helper agent and
    report back. The main thread gets the answer and stays clean. <a href={url('explain/orchestration/')}>Orchestration</a> covers how.
  </p>
  <p>
    Write things down in files. Anything that should outlive the thread, such as a decision, a plan or a list of what is left,
    belongs in a file in the project. A file survives compaction and a new thread, and any tool can read it.
  </p>

  <Note title="Before a long run">
    <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.bestPractice" show="claim" /> Most of that is context: it is what you put in the prompt and the instruction file before you leave it alone.
  </Note>

  <h2 id="how-the-model-works">How the model works</h2>
  <p>
    The board's window lab is a model, not a measurement. Window sizes, the compaction point, prices, the size of a skill
    description, the skill listing budget and the number of files re-read after compaction come from the sources listed at
    the foot of the board. Everything else is an assumption, sized the way sessions tend to run, and set here so you can
    check it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The harness's own system prompt and built-in tools: {n(HARNESS_TOKENS)} tokens.</li>
    <li>One line of an instruction file: {TOKENS_PER_LINE} tokens.</li>
    <li>A connector offers {TOOLS_PER_CONNECTOR} tools. A tool definition loaded up front costs {n(TOKENS_PER_TOOL)} tokens; a deferred one, only its name and a share of the server's instructions, {TOKENS_PER_DEFERRED_TOOL}.</li>
    <li>A skill loaded in full: {n(SKILL_BODY_TOKENS)} tokens. A file read in full: {n(FILE_TOKENS)} tokens, about 300 lines of code.</li>
    <li>An hour of steady work is {TURNS_PER_HOUR} turns. Each turn adds {n(MESSAGE_TOKENS)} tokens of messages, plus tool results of {n(RESULT_TOKENS.trimmed)} tokens when output is trimmed, {n(RESULT_TOKENS.typical)} when typical and {n(RESULT_TOKENS.whole)} when whole logs come back.</li>
    <li>A compaction leaves a summary of {n(SUMMARY_TOKENS)} tokens. In Claude Code the most recent files are read again; for Codex the model keeps only the summary.</li>
    <li>Codex's compaction point is not in the sources, so the model uses Claude Code's share of the window, {Math.round(DEFAULT_COMPACT_SHARE * 1000) / 10}%.</li>
    <li>The agent reads its files and loads its skills at the start, then works turn by turn. The cost of a turn assumes everything but that turn's new tokens is read from the cache.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The other tiles use the same sizes. The same-request examples are written to show the kind of guess an agent makes, not
    recorded from a run.
  </p>

